00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:08.807
And when we do that,
it kind of-- you

00:04:08.807 --> 00:04:11.140
can think of this picture,
the diagram, this [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:11.950
chart.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:13.666
Think of it as a flywheel.

00:04:13.666 --> 00:04:16.360
And, you know, it has got kind
of three concentric layers.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:19.250
The black circle that
you see at the center

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:22.029
is really the machine
learning aspect

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:25.990
of AI, which really enables
machines and systems to become

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:29.050
much more intelligent with
every interaction, learned

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:32.020
through every interaction that
it goes through so that it

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:33.540
can improve its performance.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.380
And today, what is
happening with AI

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:37.220
and what's going to
happen in the future,

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:38.511
I would say this is the kernel.

00:04:38.511 --> 00:04:39.370
This is the core.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.410
And a lot of investment
is happening here today.

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:42.580
A lot of advancement
is happening

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.955
in the whole machine learning,
whether it's deep learning

00:04:44.955 --> 00:04:46.217
or shallow learning, you know?

00:04:46.217 --> 00:04:47.800
This is going to be
an exciting space,

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.780
and it's getting
into mainstream.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.240
What comes on top
of the learning loop

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:55.600
is really the end-to-end
capabilities around AI.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.370
And as I mentioned,
it's a constellation

00:04:57.370 --> 00:04:59.980
of multiple
capabilities, starting

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:03.490
from speech recognition,
text recognition, those kinds

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:05.590
of capabilities,
using natural language

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:08.230
processing, natural language
generation to things

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:09.982
like machine
vision, for example,

00:05:09.982 --> 00:05:11.440
and a lot of
predictive algorithms.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:14.470
You kind of mash all of that
together powered by machine

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:16.210
learning capabilities.

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:19.090
And then on top of that, it
really enables organizations

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:21.490
to think of what's the
out of the possible?

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:25.120
How am I going to apply all
of these great capabilities

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:26.920
to solve a business problem?

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:29.210
And that's extremely important.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:31.460
And that's what we call
as the application layer.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:34.810
These application layers could
be about an intelligent agent

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:37.000
for enabling better
customer interaction

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.220
and intelligent customer
interaction or personalized

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:40.480
customer interaction.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:42.370
This could be about
creating fraud management

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:45.610
systems powered by machine
learning capabilities.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:49.450
This could be around how do
I create a next generation

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:52.840
of personalized health
care for my patients

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.540
to get the best outcomes.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:57.760
You know, it's like people
like us here in the room

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:00.370
think of different possibilities
that re-imagines the way we

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:02.620
run our business today.

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.120
And that's, in a
nutshell, how we

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:06.440
look at the whole sphere of AI.

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:13.420
And when we looked at applied
intelligence ourselves,

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:16.290
we said the vision of our
applied intelligence business

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:20.310
should be around the whole
sphere of human and machine

00:06:20.310 --> 00:06:22.860
interaction and collaboration.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.650
And today, if you look
at the newspapers,

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:27.240
every day there
is a lot of news,

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.450
positive news about how AI
is going to create new jobs,

00:06:31.450 --> 00:06:32.229
new possibilities.

00:06:32.229 --> 00:06:34.020
But there is also
another side of the news,

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:37.410
which says AI is going
to take away our jobs.

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:40.150
I think the answer lies
somewhere in the middle,

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:42.890
and we believe that
that's a big opportunity.

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:44.610
There is not so much
of a debate of how

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:48.180
to create that middle part and
how to apply AI in the business

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:51.060
to drive those outcomes and
what's the right way to start.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.310
And we believe that there
was a need for a management

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.470
guide for business
executives like all of us

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:57.780
here to chart that out.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:01.440
And we came up with this book
called "Human Plus Machine,"

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.180
which is really a
management guide to how

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:04.890
to drive the AI agenda.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:08.910
Our CTO, Paul Doherty, and our
managing director, Jim Wilson,

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:10.320
both of them penned this book.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:14.134
And it became a best seller
over the last few months.

00:07:14.134 --> 00:07:16.050
I would strongly recommend
you to kind of take

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:17.220
a look at this book.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:20.010
I mean, when I read this book,
what comes to me every time,

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:22.520
having been in practice
for the last 20 years,

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:24.690
is that AI is not
futuristic, you know?

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:27.960
It's been there for
the last 40, 50 years.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.710
And what's making it real
now is the availability

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:34.650
of computing power, the
availability of data,

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:36.470
and all the APIs that
are available today.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:38.304
That's making it real.

00:07:38.304 --> 00:07:39.720
Today, we are
looking at companies

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:42.060
who are using deep learning
and machine learning

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:45.355
capabilities to sequence the
entire human genome, you know,

00:07:45.355 --> 00:07:47.730
for coming up with personalized
medicine and personalized

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:49.290
treatment.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:52.290
At the same time, we are
looking at speech recognition

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:54.720
capabilities and
algorithms that is really

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.440
enabling the next generation
of human interaction, customer

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.870
interaction, and almost
being representing

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:02.670
the brand of the company.

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:04.980
You know, where AI is
becoming the new UI

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.020
for the whole company.

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:09.700
And this is happening
right here, right now.

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:13.200
And that's what we believe,
that AI is happening.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:17.327
It's available, it's
achievable, and it's affordable.

00:08:17.327 --> 00:08:19.410
And that's makes the whole
discussion so much more

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:19.910
exciting.

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:26.290
So some of the seminal research
that we did over the last six

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:29.320
to eight months, we interviewed
about 1,000-plus business

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:32.360
executives across the globe.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:35.140
A few key things came out, and
I want to share with this group.

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:40.360
First and foremost, 85% of the
executives that we interviewed

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:43.360
indicated that they are going to
invest in AI in the next three

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:44.270
years.

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:46.562
So obviously, a lot
of optimism around it.

00:08:46.562 --> 00:08:48.270
We did the research
around understanding.

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:52.150
You know, how major economies
and global industries

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:54.885
are kind of looking
at adopting AI.

00:08:54.885 --> 00:08:57.520
And our research
showed that applying AI

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:00.970
is going to help improve
profitability on an average

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:05.170
to about 38% when you look
at all the major industries.

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:07.270
It also showed us
that it's going

00:09:07.270 --> 00:09:11.350
to have a huge potential
boost to the economy

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:15.340
output in the order of
about 14 trillion US dollars

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:19.750
when we look at the 12 major
economies across the globe.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:25.180
What it also showed us was that
the whole labor productivity is

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:27.130
going to increase
by 40%, you know,

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:31.420
when you look at the time
horizon between now and 2035.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:33.610
And the premise was
really around AI

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:37.510
being able to help augment
the human workforce,

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:39.250
keeping people at the center.

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:42.700
But also a big focus around
the whole interaction

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.710
between human and machine,
where the machine is

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:46.960
doing a part of the
work, handing it over

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:49.280
to a human being,
and vise versa.

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:50.770
A new level of
collaboration will

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:53.440
start, which is going
to completely re-imagine

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:56.780
the future of work and
future of our workforce.

00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:59.410
And that's going to be
extremely, extremely exciting

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:03.630
and interesting, you know, as
we are going to witness that.

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:06.060
So having talked about
all of that optimism

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:08.920
and all of that potential
that AI and the capabilities

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:11.460
are bringing together,
what does that

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:15.540
pivot means to being an
intelligent enterprise, right?

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:17.410
And Al and I am
going to now spend

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:20.970
the time to talk
about the examples

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:24.330
of our different
client industries.

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:28.860
But I want to kind of hone on
some of the core principles

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:31.770
or traits that we are seeing
with a lot of the successes

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:34.890
that organizations are
having in their AI journey.

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:38.097
And these are around
the four broad teams.

00:10:38.097 --> 00:10:39.930
The first one that I
would like to highlight

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:42.990
is about how AI is
enabling companies

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:46.140
to become much more
flexible, much more agile.

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:50.040
And I call it as, you know,
re-imagining the whole business

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:53.290
process, which is not static,
which is not rule-based,

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:56.270
which is not just following the
standardization principles that

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:59.620
has happened in the 90s
and the early 2000s.

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:01.650
But really re-imagine
the entire business

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:05.340
process that I call as
living as adaptable, which

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:09.910
is going through a virtual cycle
and a constant learning loop,

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:12.120
just taking all the
signals coming in

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:14.880
from the different data
sources, and then taking

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:17.760
the actions and decisions
and constantly learning.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:20.970
And that creates a
completely new dynamism

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:23.430
around how we manage our
businesses and processes

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:25.020
globally.

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:29.460
The underlying factor around
creating a living and adaptable

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:33.690
process is really the data
and availability of data

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:35.490
and making real-time decisions.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:40.170
And that means the second big
trait for AI-led companies

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:43.140
is to become data
natives, empowering

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:47.850
all of our associates
and workforce to be savvy

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:52.470
with data, to be having access
to all the data that is needed

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:54.660
to do their jobs
better, and ability

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:58.710
to make real-time decisions with
the power of data and insights.

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:02.280
It has a huge mindset
shift that happens

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:05.520
when you move from a
process-centric organization

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:07.890
to a data-driven business model.

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:10.050
It's a different
mind shift and needs

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:13.500
a lot of cultural change, a
lot of leadership commitment,

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:15.420
really, to walk the talk.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:18.390
And then the third big area
is around the network effect.

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:20.670
And the way I characterize
network effect

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:25.290
is how you take a success
in one part of the business

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:28.530
and really democratize
that and industrialize that

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:30.540
across the entire organization.

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:32.980
Successful companies
are able to do that.

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:35.970
And when you do that,
you are following

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:39.480
the whole constant learning loop
cycle, which doesn't recognize

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:40.840
organizational boundaries.

00:12:40.840 --> 00:12:45.100
You are breaking the silos that
exist within the organization.

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:46.570
Not just within
your organization,

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:50.460
but across your extended supply
chain, what with your customers

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:52.260
as well as with your suppliers.

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.990
And that creates a completely
new set of exponential growth

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:56.580
opportunities.

00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:58.500
And when we do
all of these, it's

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:02.760
very important to really
be responsible in terms

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:06.000
of how we deploy the AI
systems into our business

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:09.310
or into our daily
lives, into our society,

00:13:09.310 --> 00:13:10.540
and so on, and so forth.

00:13:10.540 --> 00:13:12.330
And I'm going to
talk about that as we

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:13.902
go through the
presentation this is

00:13:13.902 --> 00:13:15.360
going to be a very
important aspect

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:19.350
to create trust and reduce the
skepticism and concerns that

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:21.460
are there with AI.

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:24.720
So I wanted to leave you
all with these four teams.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:28.260
And now we are going to delve
into the examples, where

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:30.820
we will touch upon this
and bring it to life.

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:33.120
And with that, I want to
welcome Al and walk us

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:34.835
through some of the examples.

00:13:34.835 --> 00:13:35.710
AL SAMBAR: Thank you.

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:36.710
Thank you, Arnab.

00:13:36.710 --> 00:13:38.440
A pleasure to be here.

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:39.660
I'm excited to talk about it.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:43.740
We're going to talk about
four different industry

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:49.080
examples of how we think about
applying applied intelligence.

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:54.350
And some in the audience
probably care about how,

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:55.530
and how we used it.

00:13:55.530 --> 00:13:59.970
And we'll definitely talk about
that and be open to some Q&amp;A

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:01.950
on the mechanics of what we do.

00:14:01.950 --> 00:14:05.240
But I would also imagine for
most of my conversations--

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:08.000
I do strategy work, so
most of my questions

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:10.280
are usually starting with a why.

00:14:10.280 --> 00:14:13.350
What's the business case
for applied intelligence?

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:15.420
What's the return on investment?

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:18.110
It's a significant investment
if your organization

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:21.120
comes from a history of
having never done it before.

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:22.680
So the examples
we're going to show,

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:25.310
and essentially all
of our examples,

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:28.290
really hit on these
five use cases.

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:30.710
So if you're out there,
charged for your team

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:33.710
with creating a business
case around investing in AI,

00:14:33.710 --> 00:14:34.580
look to these five.

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:35.538
I think you'll find it.

00:14:35.538 --> 00:14:37.790
But as a broad
coaching, I would tell

00:14:37.790 --> 00:14:40.640
you applied
intelligence is going

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:43.610
to change how our consumers
experience our product

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:44.850
and services.

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:48.080
They are going to
change what even

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:50.030
we sell as products
and services, how

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:52.662
those products and services--

00:14:52.662 --> 00:14:54.370
what products and
services we should sell

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:55.790
will be influenced by AI.

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:57.440
And then lastly,
which you're going

00:14:57.440 --> 00:14:59.690
to see a lot in the
examples, how we operate

00:14:59.690 --> 00:15:01.668
as a business because of AI.

00:15:06.050 --> 00:15:09.180
The first industry we'll pick
on is consumer engagement.

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.060
So think about
brands and retailers

00:15:11.060 --> 00:15:13.330
and how the consumers
experience us.

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:15.530
Let's set up a little
bit of the problem state.

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:18.740
Where is the industry today,
and where is it going?

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:20.150
How is technology changing it?

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:22.940
Just like we talked about
how dramatic this digital

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:27.050
revolution is, if you're
selling products, either as

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:29.300
a retailer or a brand that's
selling to the customer,

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:32.330
in this industry,
this is what you face.

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:35.670
You face an explosion
of channels,

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:38.120
not just that are new ways of
connecting to your customer

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:40.445
and sharing information
with your customer,

00:15:40.445 --> 00:15:41.820
but every one of
these channels--

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:43.370
I don't care who
your partner is.

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:45.920
Every one of these channels
now has development kits

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:47.840
and technology
available to be able,

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:49.760
in the moment of that
content being delivered,

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:51.810
take the order.

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:53.540
We, as product
companies and retailers,

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:55.040
haven't yet built
the infrastructure

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:56.796
to satisfy that order.

00:15:56.796 --> 00:15:58.670
But what the customer
sees is that the moment

00:15:58.670 --> 00:16:01.120
that they feel a
moment of desire,

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:05.500
they can reach out to somebody
they trust and say I want it.

00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:09.620
So we, as retailers,
have to adapt to this.

00:16:09.620 --> 00:16:14.040
We have to understand and
invest in how to build that.

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:16.910
But if you think about a
traditional retail or a brand

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:19.130
being able to handle
all of the choices

00:16:19.130 --> 00:16:22.340
that I have to make about what
content to put in which channel

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:24.650
and how to talk to each
individual consumer,

00:16:24.650 --> 00:16:27.200
that's overwhelming
and not doable

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:29.530
with our current
historic infrastructure.

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:35.910
So we, as retailers
or brands, grew up

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:38.850
and built companies
and business models

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:41.580
built around wholesale business
models, where I bring product

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:43.230
from manufacturer
to then consumer.

00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:45.900
But all of a sudden, if
across all of those channels

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:48.970
I'm going to have all this
persistence of relationships,

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:51.000
and I'm going to remember
who my customer is

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:53.910
in all of those moments so
I can respond appropriately,

00:16:53.910 --> 00:16:58.140
it changes the fundamental
platform of retailing.

00:16:58.140 --> 00:17:00.690
We still need all the old
platform essential elements,

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:03.030
the means of
production, the means

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:06.150
of getting that supply to
market, stores who distribute,

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:10.050
marketing to bring
the customer demand.

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:11.640
But the essence of
our relationship

00:17:11.640 --> 00:17:14.790
changes, because of all
of these digital channels.

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:16.349
So we start where--

00:17:16.349 --> 00:17:20.134
I grew up in an industry where I
could go and talk to a customer

00:17:20.134 --> 00:17:20.800
to drive growth.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:22.319
I could increase their traffic.

00:17:22.319 --> 00:17:24.569
I could increase their
conversion rate or their basket

00:17:24.569 --> 00:17:25.068
size.

00:17:25.068 --> 00:17:27.700
Those were all the
levels of the business.

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:31.300
But today, if we're going to
drive growth in our businesses,

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:34.242
we really have to move revenue
spending of that customer,

00:17:34.242 --> 00:17:35.700
and we have to do
it competitively.

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:38.640
So some of you will recognize
the LTV yield equation

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:41.310
on the lower left side, but if
you're a traditional retailer,

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:43.482
you never built your
motor to do that.

00:17:43.482 --> 00:17:47.150
So how do I start thinking
about all the revenue dollars,

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:49.970
the share of wealth that
I have with that customer,

00:17:49.970 --> 00:17:52.400
the share of occasions,
the share of use,

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:55.310
and really optimize when and
how I'm servicing and touching

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:56.030
that customer?

00:17:56.030 --> 00:18:00.185
Can't do that with just my
historic processes and systems.

00:18:00.185 --> 00:18:01.310
You really need two things.

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:05.210
You need the ability to
predict an individual customer,

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:08.000
what their desires are and
how those desires change

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:10.040
over the use occasion
and where they might

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:12.340
be at that moment in time.

00:18:12.340 --> 00:18:16.950
And most of our customers or
clients might have a Laura.

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:20.520
But we're not talking about
a Laura as a personality.

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:22.740
We're talking about
Laura J. Smith that

00:18:22.740 --> 00:18:25.490
lives on 123 Maple Drive
and goes to work here

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:27.650
and transits on this hub.

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:29.900
That is a profound
difference for the way

00:18:29.900 --> 00:18:31.460
we've built our
companies and how

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:36.380
we make decisions about how to
deploy money to drive demand.

00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:37.820
So it's profound in retail.

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:39.869
And then secondly, is
when I get that demand,

00:18:39.869 --> 00:18:42.410
and she says I have desire and
she's able to reach out to me,

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:44.550
because I've built
these digital channels,

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:46.190
how do I get that
service to her?

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:47.990
How do I engage all
my trading partners?

00:18:47.990 --> 00:18:50.000
How do I make the right
decision about what

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.400
inventory to bring her and
who should bring it to her?

00:18:52.400 --> 00:18:54.830
I can't do that
with just people.

00:18:54.830 --> 00:18:57.610
I need two super
power these people.

00:18:57.610 --> 00:18:59.360
So I'll give you a
little example of where

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:00.526
we did it with one retailer.

00:19:04.950 --> 00:19:08.760
This retailer provides
services, products and services.

00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:12.164
And when we walked
into the client,

00:19:12.164 --> 00:19:13.830
they had gone on a
little bit of hiring.

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:16.030
They were going to learn
to use data and analytics,

00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:18.210
and they had hired
some data scientists.

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:21.000
And those data scientists were
doing multivariate testing.

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:25.050
In this particular example, I'm
talking about how to market.

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:28.720
So they started with
marketing in this example.

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:31.050
And those data scientists,
each individual one

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:32.940
could do about three
multivariate tests.

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:36.960
And a good, well-run
executable multivariate test

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:40.140
would give them results
in three to six weeks

00:19:40.140 --> 00:19:42.155
depending on the
complexity of the test.

00:19:42.155 --> 00:19:44.030
And you can understand
that if you understand

00:19:44.030 --> 00:19:45.738
all those marketing
channels that we just

00:19:45.738 --> 00:19:46.996
put up on that board earlier.

00:19:46.996 --> 00:19:48.620
Think about all the
permutations if I'm

00:19:48.620 --> 00:19:50.390
going to go place
an ad on search,

00:19:50.390 --> 00:19:51.710
or I'm going to place
something on YouTube,

00:19:51.710 --> 00:19:53.330
or I'm going to place
something on Instagram,

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:54.800
or I'm going to place
something on Facebook.

00:19:54.800 --> 00:19:56.030
And then think about
making decisions

00:19:56.030 --> 00:19:57.154
across all of those things.

00:19:57.154 --> 00:19:59.730
The permutations of what content
and what decisions and what

00:19:59.730 --> 00:20:02.350
offer are huge.

00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:04.390
And we were using
data scientists

00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:08.080
to really mathematically tackle
that and scale-- tackle that

00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:12.070
at about one of those good
tests, a couple of those tests,

00:20:12.070 --> 00:20:13.516
every three to six weeks.

00:20:13.516 --> 00:20:14.890
So the reality is
we said we need

00:20:14.890 --> 00:20:17.950
to get this so you don't need
to be a data scientist to do it.

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:19.900
How might I use
applied intelligence

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:22.120
to look at all
those permutations

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:25.120
to let a person who's charged
with growing demand and growing

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:28.780
revenue yield in this market
pick the targets that she think

00:20:28.780 --> 00:20:30.210
is important for her strategy?

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:32.840
Like, how do I retain
my existing customers?

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:36.850
How do I optimize the lifetime
value of a high-value customer?

00:20:36.850 --> 00:20:39.460
How do I target cross-selling
different products?

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:41.080
How do I increase market basket?

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:43.750
Let business users ask
business questions,

00:20:43.750 --> 00:20:47.800
and let machines scale the
quality of that question

00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:50.800
by running tens of thousands
of multivariate tests,

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:53.380
then iterate across
all those permutations.

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:02.366
Once you understand that--
and I'll use marketing,

00:21:02.366 --> 00:21:04.740
because it's relatively--
understand all the permutations

00:21:04.740 --> 00:21:06.322
we have to make as
decision makers.

00:21:06.322 --> 00:21:08.780
But once you understand that
and build an organization that

00:21:08.780 --> 00:21:11.780
can start to use machine
learning and data in that way,

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:13.910
you don't just
stop at marketing.

00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:15.710
You start thinking
about, well, I

00:21:15.710 --> 00:21:18.080
might think about my product
completely different.

00:21:18.080 --> 00:21:21.410
I might test and add
unique product by market.

00:21:21.410 --> 00:21:22.880
I might modify my market.

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:25.490
I could test that product
before it ever comes.

00:21:25.490 --> 00:21:28.670
I'm certainly going to use
this for price promotion

00:21:28.670 --> 00:21:29.645
and placement.

00:21:29.645 --> 00:21:32.270
It's probably one of the most--
as we get to last mile delivery

00:21:32.270 --> 00:21:34.610
everywhere, placement
becomes a strategic place

00:21:34.610 --> 00:21:37.530
to use AI that drives the
profitability of your firm.

00:21:37.530 --> 00:21:40.640
So I used a simple example,
but hopefully the idea

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:41.300
comes across.

00:21:41.300 --> 00:21:43.010
In consumer products
and retail, it's

00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:47.060
the plethora of emerging
ways the consumer can

00:21:47.060 --> 00:21:50.180
talk to us and the need for us
to talk to them individually

00:21:50.180 --> 00:21:52.990
that requires us to
use machine learning

00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:54.790
and applied intelligence.

00:21:54.790 --> 00:21:57.310
And none of these
examples did we take away

00:21:57.310 --> 00:21:58.480
the role of the person.

00:21:58.480 --> 00:22:02.020
We completely
redefined their job.

00:22:02.020 --> 00:22:04.520
I'm going to turn it back to
you to cover the next section.

00:22:04.520 --> 00:22:04.940
ARNAB CHAKRABORTY: Thank you.

00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:05.550
Thank you, Al.

00:22:05.550 --> 00:22:07.900
That's so powerful, you know?

00:22:07.900 --> 00:22:11.590
As you talk about how we
can re-imagine the whole way

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:13.570
to engage with
customers and consumers,

00:22:13.570 --> 00:22:15.810
and we being all in this space.

00:22:15.810 --> 00:22:17.260
You know, this is amazing.

00:22:17.260 --> 00:22:18.520
I mean, the way--

00:22:18.520 --> 00:22:20.956
right now, for example,
Netflix or Amazon, the way

00:22:20.956 --> 00:22:22.330
they are engaging
with all of us.

00:22:22.330 --> 00:22:24.010
I mean, all the
investments they have

00:22:24.010 --> 00:22:26.200
done over the last
many years is really

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:29.330
acting the flywheel effect
we can start seeing, right?

00:22:29.330 --> 00:22:33.680
So yeah, thanks for
sharing that story.

00:22:33.680 --> 00:22:36.250
So I'm going to take
a little switch now

00:22:36.250 --> 00:22:40.950
from the consumer to the food
and agriculture industry.

00:22:40.950 --> 00:22:43.480
And this is something
close to me,

00:22:43.480 --> 00:22:46.560
given I am such a big foodie.

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:49.090
We are seeing a huge
application of AI

00:22:49.090 --> 00:22:51.160
in the field of agriculture.

00:22:51.160 --> 00:22:53.020
When you look at
agriculture today,

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:55.960
statistics shows that
across the world,

00:22:55.960 --> 00:23:00.310
there are over 795 million
people who don't get access

00:23:00.310 --> 00:23:02.770
to quality food.

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:05.170
As we look at the
growth of the population

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:08.080
globally, over
the next 50 years,

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:09.970
the amount of food
that has to be produced

00:23:09.970 --> 00:23:12.400
in different
agricultural farms is

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:14.590
going to be much higher
than what has happened

00:23:14.590 --> 00:23:17.440
over the last 10,000 years.

00:23:17.440 --> 00:23:22.150
And how can AI really
enable to re-imagine

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:25.180
agricultural
operations and empower

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:28.860
farmers to make decisions,
every-day decisions,

00:23:28.860 --> 00:23:31.210
with the power of intelligence
that is coming in?

00:23:31.210 --> 00:23:35.440
It's a complete change and
shift in their business model.

00:23:35.440 --> 00:23:38.590
We have been working
with this client, who's

00:23:38.590 --> 00:23:41.110
a big agricultural manufacturer.

00:23:41.110 --> 00:23:46.510
Have acres, millions of
hectares of agricultural land.

00:23:46.510 --> 00:23:48.100
And the challenge
they are faced with

00:23:48.100 --> 00:23:52.930
is a lot of the decisions
in terms of which part

00:23:52.930 --> 00:23:55.990
of the know farm with thousands
and thousands of trees

00:23:55.990 --> 00:23:57.870
and plants being grown--

00:23:57.870 --> 00:23:59.410
where to put their attention.

00:23:59.410 --> 00:24:02.200
Typically, they do a
sample size and people

00:24:02.200 --> 00:24:05.950
would go into the fields, and
they will do their samples.

00:24:05.950 --> 00:24:08.274
And they'll come back, and
then they do an analysis.

00:24:08.274 --> 00:24:10.690
Based on that, they will take
a lot of gut feel decisions.

00:24:10.690 --> 00:24:12.820
How is the weather going
to look like tomorrow?

00:24:12.820 --> 00:24:14.500
You know, what kind
of soil condition

00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:16.560
we are seeing today
in the sample set?

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:19.480
Where should I actually do
more nutrient injection?

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:21.520
Where do I need to
water my plants better?

00:24:21.520 --> 00:24:22.940
And so on, and so forth.

00:24:22.940 --> 00:24:25.020
It's a hugely manual
process, you know?

00:24:25.020 --> 00:24:27.850
And this is where we came in.

00:24:27.850 --> 00:24:30.790
And we worked with this client
over the last couple of years,

00:24:30.790 --> 00:24:31.630
initially a pilot.

00:24:31.630 --> 00:24:34.570
Now we are providing a
precision agriculture service,

00:24:34.570 --> 00:24:38.410
which is nothing but
AI at the center of it,

00:24:38.410 --> 00:24:40.240
starting with being
a data native.

00:24:40.240 --> 00:24:42.726
Being a data native,
helping the client

00:24:42.726 --> 00:24:44.350
to actually look at
data that is coming

00:24:44.350 --> 00:24:47.620
in from aerial images
with drones flying

00:24:47.620 --> 00:24:51.550
on the top of the agricultural
farmlands, taking aerial images

00:24:51.550 --> 00:24:52.420
of--

00:24:52.420 --> 00:24:55.510
to show the kind of distress
that the crops might be at,

00:24:55.510 --> 00:24:58.690
which is not possible
when you go down there.

00:24:58.690 --> 00:25:03.640
Looking at IoT devices emitting
soil chemical composition

00:25:03.640 --> 00:25:06.640
information as well
as weather information

00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:10.601
and triangulating all of that
into a cloud-based platform,

00:25:10.601 --> 00:25:12.850
applying machine learning,
and a lot of machine vision

00:25:12.850 --> 00:25:15.760
capabilities on top of
that, to really come up

00:25:15.760 --> 00:25:20.910
with predictive recommendations
that can go up to the farmer

00:25:20.910 --> 00:25:23.320
and on their mobile
device, really

00:25:23.320 --> 00:25:25.300
giving them guidance
and assistance

00:25:25.300 --> 00:25:27.970
in terms of what specific
intervention needs

00:25:27.970 --> 00:25:31.180
to be made that particular
day, that particular week,

00:25:31.180 --> 00:25:34.360
based on all the multiple
parameters that we are seeing

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:37.030
coming in from the soil
information, the weather

00:25:37.030 --> 00:25:39.400
information, the
color of the leaves,

00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:41.770
whether there is wheat
formation happening somewhere,

00:25:41.770 --> 00:25:44.110
which parts of the plantation
that's not actually

00:25:44.110 --> 00:25:45.790
growing properly,

00:25:45.790 --> 00:25:48.430
and giving very
personalized recommendation

00:25:48.430 --> 00:25:52.060
and targeted recommendation
for the farmer.

00:25:52.060 --> 00:25:53.920
Taking it one step
further, you know,

00:25:53.920 --> 00:25:57.700
integrating that intelligence
back into the work management

00:25:57.700 --> 00:26:00.340
system of the broader
agricultural company

00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:03.190
to help deployment of the right
resources in the right way,

00:26:03.190 --> 00:26:04.670
in a smarter way.

00:26:04.670 --> 00:26:09.160
And as we do that, think about
the living adaptive process

00:26:09.160 --> 00:26:10.660
that I mentioned earlier.

00:26:10.660 --> 00:26:13.930
You know, the farmer, the
agricultural operations

00:26:13.930 --> 00:26:17.080
manager, they became part
of that learning loop.

00:26:17.080 --> 00:26:19.120
And they received
the recommendation.

00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:21.820
They looked at the
recommendation, took an action.

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:23.280
The feedback was good.

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:26.370
They looped back the feedback
into the learning system.

00:26:26.370 --> 00:26:28.540
If the result was
not that great,

00:26:28.540 --> 00:26:30.220
they also provide that feedback.

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:34.390
As a result of that, the whole
precision agricultural engine

00:26:34.390 --> 00:26:36.700
started becoming smarter
over period of time.

00:26:36.700 --> 00:26:40.690
And then it scale from applying
to one particular farmland

00:26:40.690 --> 00:26:45.010
to hundreds of those farmlands
across different crop varieties

00:26:45.010 --> 00:26:46.760
over a period of six months.

00:26:46.760 --> 00:26:48.700
And that's AI at scale.

00:26:48.700 --> 00:26:51.340
You know, industrialize
across their businesses

00:26:51.340 --> 00:26:55.030
and really completely
changing a mind shift.

00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:59.190
This company came from a
mind shift of in the past,

00:26:59.190 --> 00:27:00.940
you know, they would
take more rule-based,

00:27:00.940 --> 00:27:03.430
experienced-based decisions.

00:27:03.430 --> 00:27:05.800
And today, it's all
happening with data,

00:27:05.800 --> 00:27:08.650
becoming a data native
and really living

00:27:08.650 --> 00:27:11.410
the new adaptive
agricultural process,

00:27:11.410 --> 00:27:15.100
where the farmers and the
agricultural machine operators,

00:27:15.100 --> 00:27:18.280
all of them are part of
the new AI learning system,

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:21.310
providing feedback on every
interaction, every iteration.

00:27:21.310 --> 00:27:23.030
And what does it result in?

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:25.900
It results in higher
yield of the produce,

00:27:25.900 --> 00:27:29.590
better quality, optimization
of resources, water resources,

00:27:29.590 --> 00:27:31.510
nutrients, human labor.

00:27:31.510 --> 00:27:34.060
And primarily, it's
augmenting the human

00:27:34.060 --> 00:27:37.060
to actually-- whether it's
the farmer or the operator,

00:27:37.060 --> 00:27:39.550
agricultural operator,
to make better decisions,

00:27:39.550 --> 00:27:41.680
make better profits,
serve people like you

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:44.170
and me with better
quality of food.

00:27:44.170 --> 00:27:47.950
So that's one story I wanted
to share in terms of a journey.

00:27:47.950 --> 00:27:48.820
This is going on.

00:27:48.820 --> 00:27:50.560
It's a big
transformation journey.

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:52.270
And based on the success--

00:27:52.270 --> 00:27:55.150
and I had an
opportunity of meeting

00:27:55.150 --> 00:27:56.440
the person who is running it.

00:27:56.440 --> 00:27:58.150
He's a 28-year-old guy.

00:27:58.150 --> 00:27:59.400
This is a family-run business.

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:01.420
He's the son of the
